Meaning of the `?` marker
-------------------------
In the matching 7.0.0-38.38 source, `arch/x86/kernel/dumpstack.c` documents
that the kernel scans stack words for text addresses and prints addresses not
reported by the unwinder as additional debugging clues prefixed with `?`
(source lines 239–247). The code marks a candidate reliable only when its
stack slot equals `unwind_get_return_address_ptr(&state)` (lines 249–267); an
unreliable candidate is printed but does not advance the unwinder (lines
280–293). This establishes that `?` is an unreliable diagnostic candidate,
not a verified call-chain frame. It does not establish that the candidate is
stale or false: kernel source explicitly describes these values as clues that
may be useful.

`arch/x86/kernel/unwind_orc.c` also documents one way the unwinder can become
unreliable: if ORC metadata is absent, it guesses frame-pointer unwinding and
sets the error state (lines 510–518). The saved run data contains no unwinder
state proving that this fallback occurred in either panic. Do not infer an
ORC miss from the `?` prefix alone.

Context and remaining limits
----------------------------
Both syscall journals record syscall 35 at user RIP `0x40020b` with return
value `0x0`, so a successful nanosleep occurred in each run. The scheduler and
nanosleep names are consistent with that execution context, but the journal
does not identify which raw stack word produced each candidate. In particular,
the exact provenance of the run-1-only `psi_task_switch`, `__schedule`, and
`set_ptes.isra.0` candidates remains unresolved. The `set_ptes` symbol clone
does resolve uniquely to the `mm/memory.c` instance, but the stack word's
origin is unknown. The `__pfx_hrtimer_wakeup` result is a one-past-prefix
address that resolves to a function entry; that alone does not prove how the
address entered the scanned stack.

Astra separately inspected a diagnostic UART projection after removing nonce,
timestamps, and optional-frame lines. It leaves only the already observed
memory-available and `sched_clock` calibration values, the existing adjacent
`Run /init` / `OD_NONCE` ordering difference, and user RSP. This projection is
not the strict comparator and does not satisfy acceptance.

Conclusion: all eight unknown optional strings have verified symbol/offset
resolution in the matching ELF, including the uniquely selected `set_ptes`
clone; their `?` status means the kernel did not confirm them as unwinder
return-address frames. Some function names fit the recorded nanosleep/scheduler
context, but the origin of the raw stack candidates is not recovered. There is
no evidence to add these names to `optional_panic_frame`, and no blanket `?`
normalization is justified. The
strict pair remains `matches=false` on machine observations and ordered UART;
S5-07 remains blocked/unaccepted.
